<h2>Why Compliance Matters More Than Ever</h2>
<p>The regulatory landscape for HMO landlords has tightened significantly over the past decade. Local councils are more active in enforcement, tenants are more aware of their rights, and the penalties for non-compliance — unlimited fines, civil penalties of up to £30,000, and loss of HMO licence — are more severe than ever. Staying on top of your compliance obligations isn't optional; it's fundamental to operating a lawful and profitable rental property.</p>

<h2>Fire Risk Assessment</h2>
<p>A formal fire risk assessment carried out by a qualified assessor is mandatory for all HMOs. It must identify fire hazards, evaluate the risk posed to occupants, and set out the control measures in place. The assessment should be reviewed annually and updated whenever material changes are made to the property. Without a current fire risk assessment, your HMO licence is at risk.</p>

<h2>Gas Safety Certificate</h2>
<p>Any property with gas appliances must have an annual Gas Safety Check carried out by a Gas Safe registered engineer. The resulting certificate — known as a CP12 — must be provided to all tenants within 28 days of the check, and a copy must be kept for at least two years. Failure to comply is a criminal offence.</p>

