<h2>Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR)</h2>
<p>Since 2020, all HMO landlords in England have been required to have the electrical installation inspected and tested by a qualified electrician. The EICR must be renewed every five years, or sooner if the report identifies remedial work. A copy must be provided to all tenants and to the local authority on request.</p>

<h2>PAT Testing</h2>
<p>Portable Appliance Testing (PAT) covers all electrical appliances provided by the landlord — kettles, toasters, washing machines, microwaves, and any other plug-in devices. While there is no fixed legal interval, regular PAT testing (typically annually for HMOs) is considered best practice and is required by many local authority licensing conditions.</p>

<h2>Asbestos Management Survey</h2>
<p>Any property built before 2000 may contain asbestos-containing materials (ACMs). As a landlord, you have a duty to manage any ACMs in your property. This begins with an asbestos management survey to identify the location, condition, and type of any ACMs present. If ACMs are in good condition and unlikely to be disturbed, they can often be left in place and monitored. Damaged or friable ACMs must be remediated by licensed contractors.</p>

<h2>Legionella Risk Assessment</h2>
<p>The Health and Safety at Work Act requires all landlords to assess the risk of Legionella bacteria in their water systems. For most residential HMOs, this means a periodic risk assessment by a competent person, checking for conditions that promote Legionella growth — stagnant water, temperatures between 20–45°C, and scale or debris in tanks and pipes. Simple control measures such as flushing infrequently used outlets and setting water heaters above 60°C are usually sufficient.</p>

<h2>Damp Survey</h2>
<p>Damp and mould are among the most common complaints in rented properties and have become the focus of increased regulatory attention following high-profile cases in England. A professional damp survey identifies the cause — rising damp, penetrating damp, or condensation — and recommends appropriate remediation. Addressing damp promptly protects the structure of your property and the health of your tenants.</p>

